<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key Insights from Implem=
entation:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance was critica=
l</strong> - Permission checks happened on every render, so we <strong>memo=
ized permission calculations</strong> and used React Context with careful o=
ptimization</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>User experience mattere=
d</strong> - Instead of hiding features, we showed them in disabled states =
with explanatory tooltips</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Real-time updates</stro=
ng> - Permissions could change during a session (e.g., trading hours ending=
), so we needed <strong>WebSocket updates for permission states</strong></l=
i>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The most challenging aspect was =
<strong>handling optimistic updates with permission validation</strong>. Fo=
r example, if a user initiated a trade but their permissions changed mid-fl=
ight due to account limits, we needed to gracefully handle that.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's exactly the kind of complexity we're dealing with at Webflow, but acro=
ss visual design tools rather than financial data. Let me dig deeper into y=
our React patterns. How do you approach component design and reusability wh=
en you're building complex, interactive interfaces?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Comp=
onent design is where I think <strong>functional programming principles rea=
lly shine in React</strong>. I've evolved my approach over the years, and h=
ere's my current philosophy:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Design Philosophy: Compo=
sition over Configuration</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Instead of creating monolithic c=
omponents with lots of props, I prefer <strong>smaller, composable pieces</=
strong> that can be combined in different ways.</p>
