<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance Consideratio=
ns</strong>: We implemented <strong>code splitting at the route and compone=
nt level</strong>, reducing initial bundle size by 60%. Critical insight wa=
s that financial dashboards have <strong>distinct usage patterns</strong> -=
 users either want quick overview or deep analysis, rarely both simultaneou=
sly.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: In=
teresting approach with the layered state management. I'm curious about the=
 authorization aspects since that's what you'd be working on here. How did =
you handle permissions in that financial application?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question - permissions in fintech are incredibly nuanced because they inv=
olve both <strong>regulatory compliance and user experience considerations<=
/strong>. We had several layers:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Permission Architecture:=
</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Role-based permissions<=
/strong> (Advisor, Client, Compliance Officer)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Resource-level permissi=
ons</strong> (Account access, transaction limits)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Time-based permissions<=
/strong> (Trading hours, approval windows)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Regulatory permissions<=
/strong> (Accredited investor status, jurisdiction-based features)</li>
</ol>
